* the thread-specific playground:

int32_t dlock; /* daemon lock */
int32_t tlock; /* thread lock */
int32_t tip;   /* thread is in a syscall prolog */
int32_t count; /* number of pending signals */



* thread prolog

1) atomically set tip to 1;
2) 64-bit locked cas, {0,0} --> {0,1}
   success: enter system call
   failure: daemon lock has been acquired, process signals (the faster way)
            (if the daemon lock is 2 rather than 1, then it is the
             thread's responsibility to reset the lock)

* thread epilog
1) process any pending signals (the faster way)
2) atomically set (64-bit) tlock and tip to zero



* daemon signal delivery

1) find a thread for which the signal may be delivered

2) increase the number of pending signals

3) queue user apc

4) acquire daemon lock (always succeeds)

5) see whether a thread lock had also been acquired

   yes: we are done, since the thread will process the signals
        the faster way in the epilog

   no:  suspend the thread

6) see whether the thread is in the middle of a system call
   prolog (tip)

   yes: increase the daemon lock (from 1 to 2)
	wake the thread;
	wait for the thread do reset the daemon lock
	we are done, since the thread will now process
	the signals the faster way;

   no:	do the thing
